\}})` lazy match returned "" — the lazy engine finds the shortest match: the 
}} might be immediately at capture start? No... body has no "
}}" in my test string! I forgot }} in the test. But it still returned "" with alternation m[2]=""? For `withAlt`, alternation m[2] should capture rest of the body — but got ""? Lazy `(.*)` — `.` doesn't match newlines, so m[2] = "" (empty to end of line after "location ="). And the strict one fails because no 
}} exists in test body. So in the real data it should capture the multiline list... but in the real page the actualREGEX gave empty. Because my code does `m[1].match(/
\|\s*location\s*=([\s\S]*?)(?=
\}})/)` — the body DOES end with "
}}"? The box regex consumed the terminator 
}} as part of `
\}\}` pattern... In regex `\{\{MitamaBox([\s\S]*?)
\}\}` — the terminator 
}} is exactly the end; the lookahead can't find ANOTHER 
}} after the capture since body string lacks the terminator (it was consumed). Lazy would extend to end of string (no positional barrier) — with `(?=
\}})` never satisfiable... but lazy engines backtrack: capture grows to end of body; lookahead fails; overall match fails → location undefined → "".

