Hmm — Iwanaga Hime: the box measures 380×380, but the visible figure is still small because the source art (1160×1160) has huge transparent margins — the actual sprite occupies maybe 35% of the canvas width. Even at 380px box the visible figure ≈ 150px. The user's complaint is precisely this: photo looks small. Increasing the box to 380 helped a bit, but the transparent canvas eats it.

Hmm — "for large photo size could you increase size" — they want the photo bigger. For 608 the figure stays small-ish due to the source data. That's the earlier "known data reality" (heroFit preserves natural aspect; art has transparent margins).

But wait — the visible render box is fine; can we be smarter? We could scale by measuring the painted (non-transparent) bounding box? That needs canvas analysis — could compute alpha bounding box client-side once per art, then size the photo so the VISIBLE figure reaches the box height. That's a real improvement for transparent-canvas arts, and testable on the two entries only. That might be what the user actually wants: bigger-looking photos.

Scope: user asked to increase size for test with those two. Current test delivers 380px box. The Iwanaga result still looks smallish (sprite ~180px?). In the screenshot the visible figure is roughly 220px wide of 1130px modal. Snow Spirit's art has less transparent margin? Its box 340×380 from a 260×291 image — the visible ice-spirit probably fills most of it.

Simplest response: show the test results, and note the Iwanaga source canvas has big transparent margins so the visible figure gains proportionally; offer the "alpha-trim" enhancement (measure non-transparent bounding box and size to it) as the next step if it still looks small.

Actually — implementing alpha-trim only for these two test entries is doable: in heroFit, if img has data-hero-trim, draw to offscreen canvas at natural size, scan alpha, compute bbox, then scale so bbox height → maxH, and set width/height accordingly with the visible figure fitting 380. That directly answers "large photo" per-test.

Given the user's test intent, I think delivering the alpha-trim for these two is the RIGHT response to their complaint (they've seen small photos repeatedly — earlier reviews noted "hero art is tiny"). Let me implement trim in heroFit when dataset.heroTrim is present:

```js
function heroFit(img) {
  const maxW = Number(img.dataset.heroW) || 340, maxH = Number(img.dataset.heroH) || 250;
  if (!img.naturalWidth || !img.naturalHeight) return;
  let w = img.naturalWidth, h = img.naturalHeight;
  ...fit s...
  img.style.width → w*s etc.
  if (img.dataset.heroTrim === "1" && !img.dataset.trimmed) { compute bbox via canvas → adjust }
}
```

Implementation detail: cross-origin? Local images, same origin (http://localhost:8123) → canvas getImageData OK. On file:// would taint? gates don't run heroFit on real pages. Fine for http.

Algorithm:
```js
function trimBox(img) {
  const c = document.createElement("canvas");
  c.width = img.naturalWidth; c.height = img.naturalHeight;
  const ctx = c.getContext("2d"); ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  try { const d = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, c.width, c.height).data;
    let x0 = c.width, y0 = c.height, x1 = 0, y1 = 0;
    for (let y = 0; y < c.height; y++) {
      for (let x = 0; x < c.width; x++) {
        if (d[(y * c.width + x) * 4 + 3] > 8) {
          if (x < x0) x0 = x; if (x > x1) x1 = x;
          if (y < y0) y0 = y; if (y > y1) y1 = y;
        }
      }
    }
    if (x1 <= x0 || y1 <= y0) return null;
    return { w: x1 - x0 + 1, h: y1 - y0 + 1 };
  } catch { return null; }
}
```

Then in heroFit with trim: instead of fitting the whole canvas, fit so the VISIBLE portion reaches (maxW, maxH): scale s = min(maxW / bw, maxH / bh)... but then placed size = natural*s may exceed the layout box (overflow). Set overflow visible? The head has align-items center… the img element sized to natural*s could overflow the modal column (md-head avatar). Layout width: md-head column max ~ (modal 760 − padding 2rem*2) = 696; img width 1160*0.327=380 ✓ fits. For safety cap by natural size: s = min(maxW/bw, maxH/bh, maxLayoutW/w?). The img BOX = w×h natural scaled; visible figure centered inside. 1160×1160 at s=... bh maybe ~700 → s=380/700=0.543 → img box 630×630!! Too big box, visible 380 — box would blow past modal width (696) barely OK but layout height 630 huge → modal head grows.

Better: keep img element sized to visible fit and shift the CONTENT: use object-fit/… we can't crop source transparent margins via CSS without object-view-box (Chrome supports object-view-box: inset(top right bottom left))! Chrome-only but our env is Chrome/Edge. object-view-box with inset percentages crops the canvas to the bbox; combined with width/height element size = visible dimensions scaled. But then if the element is exactly bbox-fitted, that's ideal: set width = bw*s, height = bh*s, and object-view-box inset right etc.

However object-view-box on <img>: the natural canvas is scaled with object-fit... object-view-box specifies the source rectangle in view box units. Support: Chrome 104+, Edge 104+. Firefox/Safari ignore → falls back to whole canvas shown small — acceptable graceful degradation? If unsupported, art shows as before (smaller). Our site targets Chrome/Edge anyway (self-contained local archive).

Alternatively simpler: canvas-trim once and replace src with cropped dataURL! Actually that's robust cross-browser: after load, crop to bbox, set img.src = cropped dataURL, background none, then heroFit fits that. Downside: memory fine for a couple test images. Also transparent background preserved (PNG). This works everywhere.

I'll implement trimData for entries with data-hero-trim, converting the src once (in heroFit when dataset.heroTrim === "1" and not already trimmed):

```js
async function heroFit(img) {
  const maxW..., maxH...
