Data facts:
- Corners fully transparent ✓
- bbox stays ~1150×780 across ALL thresholds (even alpha>60 and Vis40) — the content genuinely extends to the edges: the opaque a255 pixels = 201,500, only 2,247 of them near-black. So the visible-content bbox really is ~1150 wide. The art (with wings spread) spans nearly the full canvas edge-to-edge.

Wait — then why does the render 640×442 show the figure only ~400px? Look at the screenshot again... the img element: box 640 wide... The visible art spans 330-730 within the modal, but where's the element? Modal content: 202-1078. If img flex-start at 202: 202-842. So visible art inside 330-730 means within the img only central 400px are visibly occupied despite bbox being full width.

Hmm — actually no. Look at the screenshot yet again. The figure with halo and wings DOES span from ~330 to ~730 = 400px. And 640 * (1150/1151)... the wings visually span the full trim box?? The crop was y 184-979, x 5-1156. Rendered at 640/1151 = 0.556: the visible wings at 400px wide correspond to source 720px. So the wings are only ~720/1150 = 63% of the trim width.

But bbox says content at x≈5..1156 — meaning there ARE alpha>8 pixels near edges. Conclusion: those edge pixels are ultra-faint (a1_8 bucket = 16,802 px + a9_26 12,420 + a27_60 14,174). Very sparse faint scatter — like 1-2px stray dots or MM dust near edges, or extremely faint glow.

Since bbox barely changes with Vis40 (1147), the near-edge pixels even at higher thresholds: at threshold Vis40 the bbox x0=9, still near edge! There must be a few solidish pixel clusters far left/right. Could be decorative particles (the little 雪/hearts floating outward) — the art does have small ornaments (two golden charms at left ~370 and right ~630 in screenshot — wait, those ornaments ARE within the figure region... hmm the screenshot at 330-730 showed ornaments INSIDE the figure zone. So content beyond 730: invisible-ish particles/dust with alpha>40.

Realistically: those edge pixels are sparse particles (the sparkle dots in the screen, maybe at low alpha but some >60).

Since the trim must be visual: what we need is bbox of the *dense* content. Approach: percentile-based row/column occupancy — a row participates only if ≥ N pixels of visible content in it. E.g., per column count alpha>26 strong pixels; column participates if count ≥ 3 (ignores 1-2 dot noise). Simpler: compute for each column the count of solid pixels (alpha>100); keep columns with count ≥ 4.

Risk for souls (small icons ~260px): legends: solid = alpha>100: fine, icons are dense solid; y-edge anti-aliased edges count<threshold → crop pad 6 keeps.

This is getting heavy. Alternative pragmatic approach: forget smarter trim; user wants layout: "text closer to photo". Even with bbox ~1150, panels: we render 640 wide. The perceived gap: text starts after img element (640 wide). The img element itself has invisible left part? Wait — in the screenshot the figure is at 330-730, so the img element spanned 202-842?? Then the render center: figure center ~530, element center 522 ✓ centered-ish within element. So element occupies 202-842 with figure solid 330-730 (128px invisible left, 112px invisible right), then text at ~856+.

If we trim to dense bbox (720 source px wide at scale: rendered 640 box: height-limited? dense 741×780 → fit 640×500: s = min(640/741, 500/780)=min(0.864,0.641)=0.641 → 475×500. Text right after → art 475 wide, figure fills entire box → text adjacent. Modal content 876 wide: art 475 + gap 22 + text ~370 fits!

So dense-bbox trim solves it. Implement: threshold alpha>26 (visibility) require count per column ≥ 3 and per row ≥ 3? Then pad 6.

Actually simpler global rule: two passes — find bbox of pixels alpha>26, then compute occupancy histogram and shrink bounds where occupancy < 3. Implement in one pass: colDensity[x], rowDensity[y]; first compute bbox edges via scanning outward: from x=0, find first x where colDensity[x] ≥ 3; similarly from right, top, bottom.

Pseudo:
```
const vis = (a) => a > 26;
// density arrays
const col = new Uint32Array(W), row = new Uint32Array(H);
loop: if (vis(alpha)) { col[x]++, row[y]++ }
// bounding: start=first index with density>=2, end=last with density>=2
```
That's O(W*H) single full loop regardless — fine (1160² = 1.3M — quick).

Edge case: souls small icons: most columns dense; fine.

Wait, actually — I should double check where the extra width comes from: perhaps the spread wings tip feathers do reach near x edges but with alpha 27-60 (faint). Vis40 bbox = 1147 — hmm that still contradicts "wings only 720 wide". Vis40 bbox x0=9: SOMETHING at x=9 has alpha>40. Sparkles. Density approach will tell: at x near 0, density probably 1-2 pixels only → excluded.

